        <div id="need-60" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Do You Need a 60-Inch Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            It depends on how fast you go and how tall you are. Walking strides are short, so a 50 to 55-inch belt is
            enough for most walkers. Running strides get longer as you speed up, and a treadmill belt also needs room
            for you to drift forward and back.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">You mostly…</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Belt length to look for</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">50–55 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Jog</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">55 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Run</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">60 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Run fast or are over about 6 ft</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">60 in., and consider 22 in. wide</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt size guide</a> gives more detail by height and speed, and our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for tall runners</a> focuses on the tallest users.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>20 or 22 Inches Wide?</h2>
<p>
            Most 60-inch home treadmills are 20 inches wide. A 22-inch belt, as on the <a href="/horizon-7-4-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Horizon 7.4 AT</a>, NordicTrack
            Commercial 1750 and Spirit XT485, adds room to drift sideways, which matters for broad shoulders, people who
            swing their arms wide, and anyone watching a screen while running. Horizon describes 22 by 60 inches as the
            commercial-gym standard.
          </p>
          <p>
            The cost of width is price and size. In our picks, the step from a 20-inch <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Horizon 7.0 AT</a> to the 22-inch 7.4
            AT is about $565 on Amazon, and the frame is wider: Horizon gives 35 inches wide for the 7.0 AT and 37 for the
            7.4. Our <a href="/best-wide-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best wide treadmills</a> compares wider decks in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Much Room a 60-Inch Treadmill Needs</h2>
<p>
            A 60-inch deck means a long machine: Horizon gives 76 inches of length for its 7.0 and 7.4 AT, and the T202
            listing gives 76 by 34 by 57 inches assembled. Add clearance behind the belt for safety and room beside it to
            step off. All seven picks fold, which helps with floor space but not height: Horizon gives folded heights of
            68 inches for the 7.0 AT and 71 for the 7.4.
          </p>
          <p>
            Measure the room, the doorway and the ceiling before you buy. Our <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill dimensions guide</a> sets out the clearances, and our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-small-spaces/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for small spaces</a> covers shorter machines if 76 inches will not fit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Motor and Speed for Running</h2>
<p>
            A long deck is only half of a running treadmill. All seven picks reach 12 mph on the specifications we found,
            except the Spirit XT485, whose page we could not read a reliable speed from, so check it with Spirit. Look at
            the continuous-duty motor rating where it is given (Horizon lists 3.5 CHP for the 7.4 AT on Amazon) rather than
            a peak figure. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> explains why, and our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for running</a> ranks machines on deck, motor and frame together.
          </p>
        </div>

