          <p>
            Two patterns stand out. First, a 60-inch deck no longer means a premium price: three of the seven were under
            $1,100 when we checked. Second, the jump to a 22-inch width comes with a higher capacity on every machine here,
            so heavier runners get both together from the Horizon 7.4 AT upward.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which 60-Inch Treadmill for Your Budget</h2>
<h3>Under $1,000</h3>
          <p>
            The <a href="/horizon-t202-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Horizon T202</a> and Horizon 7.0 AT. The T202 is the cheaper of the two and folds with a hydraulic assist, but
            Horizon gives it one year on parts and labour and its incline stops at 12%. The 7.0 AT costs about $135 more
            on the prices we saw and adds 15 incline levels, three years on parts, and Apple Watch and GymKit support. For
            most runners who will use the machine for years, the longer parts cover alone makes the 7.0 AT the better buy.
          </p>
          <h3>$1,000 to $1,500</h3>
          <p>
            The Sole F63 and F65, and the Horizon 7.4 AT at the top of the range. The two Soles share a 20 by 60-inch deck
            and suit buyers who do not want a subscription; the F65 is the one to choose over 325 lb. The 7.4 AT is the
            only machine in this bracket with a 22-inch belt, and it includes a chest strap and heart-rate programs.
          </p>
          <h3>Over $1,500</h3>
          <p>
            The NordicTrack Commercial 1750 if you want decline, a large touchscreen and iFIT classes, remembering that the
            full class library is a monthly cost on top. The Spirit XT485 if you are near or above 400 lb, or want the
            longest parts warranty here. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for heavy people</a> covers high-capacity machines in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Folding a 60-Inch Treadmill: What to Expect</h2>
<p>
            All seven picks fold, but a folding 60-inch treadmill is still a large, heavy machine. Horizon gives 277 lb for
            the 7.0 AT and 318 lb for the 7.4 AT, and their folded heights are 68 and 71 inches. That is light enough for
            the deck to lift on a gas or hydraulic assist, but not light enough to move room to room without the transport
            wheels and some care.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Folding saves floor length, not height.</strong> Check the folded height against any shelf, window
            or sloped ceiling.</li>
            <li><strong>Leave room to lower it.</strong> The deck swings down through its full length, so the space in front
            of the folded machine has to be clear.</li>
            <li><strong>Doorways matter at delivery.</strong> Most of these ship in a large carton by freight. Measure stair
            turns and doors before ordering.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you need to fold and move the treadmill daily, a shorter, lighter machine may suit better: see our <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best folding treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warranty Terms Compared</h2>
<p>
            Warranty cover differs more than the decks do, and parts cover is the figure that tends to matter most for
            repair bills. Here is what each maker states, as summarised in the picks below.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Horizon T202:</strong> lifetime frame and motor, one year parts and labour.</li>
            <li><strong>Horizon 7.0 AT:</strong> lifetime frame and motor, three years parts.</li>
            <li><strong>Horizon 7.4 AT:</strong> lifetime frame and motor, five years parts, two years labour.</li>
            <li><strong>Sole F63 and F65:</strong> lifetime frame and motor; parts and labour terms are not stated on the
            product pages, so ask Sole before buying.</li>
            <li><strong>Spirit XT485:</strong> lifetime frame, motor and deck, ten years parts, one year labour.</li>
            <li><strong>NordicTrack Commercial 1750:</strong> two years parts, per the pick below; check NordicTrack&rsquo;s
            terms for the frame and motor.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Warranties usually require registration and purchase from an authorised seller, and some exclude commercial
            use. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-long-warranty/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with the longest warranties</a> compares terms across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-60" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Can Skip a 60-Inch Deck</h2>
<p>
            A 60-inch deck costs more, weighs more and takes more floor space, so it is worth being honest about whether
            you need one. You can usually save money with a shorter deck if:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>You walk, and do not plan to run.</strong> Walking strides are short. A 55-inch deck leaves room to
            spare for most walkers, and many good walking treadmills are cheaper and lighter.</li>
            <li><strong>You walk at a desk.</strong> A walking pad or under-desk treadmill is built for slow, steady
            walking; see our <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Space is the limit.</strong> If a 76-inch machine will not fit with safe clearance behind it, a
            shorter deck you can use safely is better than a long one squeezed in.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            You should not skip it if you run regularly, are tall, or share the treadmill with someone who does. Running on
            a deck that is too short makes you shorten your stride and risks stepping off the back, which is the reason
            the 60-inch standard exists. If you are unsure, our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a> matches deck length to height and speed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills hands-on. Each pick had to have a 60-inch running surface confirmed by its
            maker or listing, be sold on Amazon under a listing we could match, and come from a brand with published
            warranty terms. We then picked across price and width. Specifications come from Horizon&rsquo;s comparison
            tables (21 November), Sole&rsquo;s product pages (22 November), Spirit&rsquo;s XT485 page (21 November), the Amazon
            listings and our own reviews. Our <a href="/editorial-standards/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">editorial standards</a> describe the wider process.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Before You Buy a 60-Inch Treadmill: 4 Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from buying the deck and forgetting the machine around it.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Measuring the deck, not the room</h3>
              <p>A 60-inch deck sits in a frame about 76 inches long, and you need clearance behind it. Measure the floor, doorways and ceiling before ordering.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring width</h3>
              <p>Length is not the only fit. If you are broad or drift sideways, a 22-inch belt is more comfortable than 20.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for 60 inches to walk</h3>
              <p>If you only walk, a 55-inch belt is enough, and cheaper and smaller treadmills will do the job.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Skipping the motor and warranty</h3>
              <p>A long deck on a weak motor or short warranty is not a running treadmill. Check the continuous motor rating and the parts and labour terms too.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For a 60-inch deck, buy the Horizon 7.0 AT</strong> for value (about $935), the T202 for
            the cheapest fold (about $800), or the 7.4 AT for a 22-inch-wide deck (about $1,500). The Sole F63 and F65 add
            a subscription-free option, and the Spirit XT485 suits the heaviest users.
          </p>
          <p>
            Measure your room for a 76-inch machine and check the motor and warranty, not just the deck. Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">belt size guide</a> helps you decide whether you need 60 inches at all.
          </p>
